  "query_preview": "..\nA few widely isolated showers will be possible this afternoon and \ntonight, otherwise dry weather is expected through Labor Day.\n\nOur region will continue to be sandwiched in between a stalled upper \nlow spinning over Newfoundland/Nova Scotia and a stout upper level \nridge dominating over the central CONUS through Sunday morning, \nwhile surface high pressure just south of James Bay will continue to \nbuild south into the upper Great Lakes tonight. Our area will \ncontinue to be on the western fringe of the upper low, thus anytime \na trough pinwheeling about the upper low moves over or near our \narea, there will be at least a small chance for and isolated shower. \nThis afternoon the best chance for a shower looks to be across the \neastern Lake Ontario region as a weak shortwave moves by just to the \nnortheast. \n\nCool advection will continue into tonight with 850 mb temps getting \ndown around +9. With lake temps still sitting around +22/+23, this \nwill be enough to elicit a weak lake response overnight, mainly on \nLake Erie as a convergent NE to ENE flow sets up on Lake Erie with \nmost of the activity expected to remain offshore. A N to NNE cross-\nlake flow over Lake Ontario will also produce some lake clouds south \nof the Lake, and with the added boost from upslope flow, may see a \nfew lake enhanced scattered showers develop over the higher terrain \nof the Finger Lakes overnight. Otherwise, most of the area remains \ndry with comfortable sleeping weather. \n\nThe upper low will finally begin to pull away later Sunday and \nSunday night, while the upper level ridge axis builds a bit further \neast into the upper Great Lakes. Surface high pressure over the \nupper Great Lakes tonight will build south into the lower Great \nLakes region for Sunday and Labor Day. This will end any lingering \nshower chances, with dry weather expected for the remainder of the \nlong holiday weekend.",
